# CHAPTER II

## PROTECTION OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN

## Part One

Rights of Women Article 4 Every Woman has the right:
a. to live, sustain life and her existence; 
b. to form a family and continue offspring through lawful marriage;  
c. to develop herself through the fulfillment of her basic needs; 
d. to obtain education and benefit from science and technology, arts and culture, in order to improve the quality of her life and for the welfare of humankind;  
e. to advance herself in fighting for her rights collectively to build society, nation and state;  
f. to recognition, guarantee, protection and fair legal certainty as well as equal treatment before the law;  
g. to work and receive fair and proper remuneration and treatment in employment relations;  
h. to obtain equal opportunities in government;  
i. to freedom of association, assembly and expression of opinion;  

j. to protection of her person, family, honor, dignity and property under her control, and the right to a sense of security and protection from threats of fear to do or not do something which is a basic right;  
k. to be free from torture or treatment that degrades human dignity and the right to obtain political asylum from other countries;  
l. to live in physical and spiritual prosperity, to reside and obtain a good and healthy living environment, and the right to obtain health services;  

m. to social security that enables her full development as a dignified human being;  
n. to equality of position in law and government;  
o. to work and a livelihood worthy of humanity;  
p. in efforts to defend the state;  
q. to communicate and obtain information to develop her personality and social environment, and the right to seek, obtain, possess, store, process, and convey information using all types of available channels;  
r. to embrace her respective religion and to worship according to her religion and belief; and 
s. to obtain education.

## Part Two

Fulfillment of Children's Rights

### Paragraph 1

Rights and Obligations of Children Article 5 Every Child has the right:
a. to birth registration;  
b. to obtain health;  
c. to obtain education; and 
d. to social welfare.  

Article 6 Every Child has the obligation to:
a. respect parents, guardians and teachers;  
b. love family, community and care for friends; 
c. love the homeland, nation and state;  
d. perform worship in accordance with his/her religion and belief;  
e. study and develop himself/herself in accordance with his/her abilities and talents, interests; and 
f. have noble character.  

Paragraph 2 Birth Registration

#### Article 7

Birth registration of children as referred to in Article 5 letter a, the Regency Government is obliged to issue a birth certificate in accordance with the provisions of Laws and Regulations.

### Paragraph 3

Health Article 8 (1) The implementation of Fulfillment of Children's Rights in the field of health as referred to in Article 5 letter b, the Regency Government is obliged to provide facilities and organize comprehensive health services for children.  

2. Comprehensive health services as referred to in paragraph (1) include:
a. promotive, preventive, curative and rehabilitative efforts for basic health services; and
b. exemption from cost burden for malnourished children, Children with disabilities, children with special needs, street children, children infected with HIV/AIDS, child laborers, children victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic and other addictive substances (NAPZA) abuse, Children who are victims of violence, such as neglect, economically and sexually exploited and victims of human trafficking.  
3. Exemption from cost burden for Children as referred to in paragraph (2) letter b is for Children from poor families unless otherwise regulated in accordance with the health financing program established by the government.  

Article 9 The Regency Government together with the community, family and parents are obliged to endeavor to ensure that Children who are born are protected from diseases that threaten the continuity of life and/or cause disability.

### Paragraph 4

Education Article 10 (1) The implementation of Fulfillment of Children's Rights in the field of education as referred to in Article 5 letter c, the Regency Government guarantees the implementation of a 9 (nine) year compulsory education program.  

2. Every organizer of an education unit is obliged to provide opportunities for Children to obtain educational services without discrimination.  

Article 11 Every organizer of an education unit is prohibited from expelling Children from educational institutions unless there is a guarantee for the continuity of the Child's education.  

Article 12 Every organizer of an education unit is obliged to coordinate with relevant child protection agencies if they find Children dropping out of school due to being victims of acts of violence.  

Article 13 Children with disabilities and Children with special needs are given equal opportunities to obtain educational services:
a. formal;  
b. non-formal;  
c. informal;  
d. special education; and 
e. inclusion.  

Article 14 Early Childhood Education aged 0 (zero) to 3 (three) years and 4 (four) to 6 (six) years may be organized by:
a. Posyandu institutions;  
b. Posyandu gardens;  
c. Early Childhood Education Posts; and 
d. similar Early Childhood Education unit institutions.

### Paragraph 5

Social Welfare Article 15 (1) The implementation of Protection and Empowerment of Children in the field of social welfare as referred to in Article 5 letter d includes:
a. children in conflict with the law;  
b. child victims of violence who are economically and sexually exploited;  
c. abandoned children;  
d. child victims of abduction and child victims of human trafficking;  
e. child victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic and other addictive substances (NAPZA) abuse;  
f. children infected with HIV/AIDS;  
g. children who do not have parents and are cared for by other parties or families who are unable;  
h. street children;  
i. child victims of natural disasters or social disasters; 
j. children with physical and mental disabilities;  
k. children of migrant worker families;  
l. children living in or around prostitution locations; and 
m. child victims of other maltreatment.  
2. Social welfare as referred to in paragraph (1) may be in the form of ease of access to services:
a. health;  
b. education;  
c. legal aid;  
d. assistance;  
e. social, mental and spiritual guidance;  
f. social rehabilitation;  
g. empowerment;  
h. social assistance; and/or 
i. reintegration.  
3. Special infrastructure for persons with disabilities in public places.

