#### Article 16

1. The Regency Government is obligated to protect the fulfillment of the rights of Child workers in the informal sector.  
2. Child workers in the informal sector as referred to in paragraph (1) include:  
   a. domestic workers;  
   b. shoe shiners;  
   c. street vendors;  
   d. scavengers;  
   e. parking attendants;  
   f. children who work in the fields of agriculture, animal husbandry and plantations;  
   g. children who work in the mining sector; and  
   h. other informal sector work that employs Children.  
3. Every person who employs Children in the informal sector must observe the following requirements:  
   a. minimum age of 15 (fifteen) years, except for the category of Worst Forms of Child Labor minimum 18 (eighteen) years;  
   b. obtain written consent from the parents/guardian of the Child worker;  
   c. have a written employment agreement between the employer and the parents/guardian of the Child worker and obtain validation from the authorized agency;  
   d. not be employed at night;  
   e. working time of at most 3 (three) hours per day;  
   f. not be employed in a place or environment that can disrupt the child's growth and development process, whether physical, mental, moral and intellectual or the Child's health;  
   g. provide opportunities to receive education in accordance with their talents and interests;  
   h. employ for light types of work; and  
   1. provide one day off per week.  

#### Article 17

Fulfillment of the rights of Child workers in the informal sector as referred to in Article 16 paragraph (1) includes:

a. counseling to stakeholders about the rights of Children;  
b. psychological, medical and legal assistance and socio-economic reintegration;  
c. family empowerment through the provision of training, business capital stimulus and assistance;  
d. scholarships to continue formal education to a higher level; and  
e. non-formal education and skills training for those who do not pursue formal education.  

## Part Three

#### Article 18

Forms of violence against Women and Children include:  

a. physical violence;  
b. psychological violence;  
c. sexual violence;  
d. neglect;  
e. exploitation; and/or  
f. other violence.  

## Part Four

#### Article 19

(1) Efforts to prevent violence against Women and Children are carried out in an integrated manner by the Regency Government and coordinated by the technical Regional Apparatus.  

(2) Prevention efforts as referred to in paragraph (1) are implemented by:  
   a. conducting guidance towards harmonious household integrity;  
   b. forming a network in efforts to prevent violence;  
   c. conducting coordination, integration, synchronization of violence prevention based on partnership patterns;  
   d. establishing a violence prevention system;  
   e. conducting socialization regarding laws and regulations related to the protection of Women and Children victims of violence; and  
   f. providing critical education regarding the rights of Women and Children for the community.  

#### Article 20

Efforts to prevent violence against Women and Children as referred to in Article 19 paragraph (1) in addition to the Regency Government are also implemented by:

a. family and/or closest relatives;  
b. community;  
c. educational institutions; and  
d. Non-Governmental Organizations.

## Part Five

#### Article 21

Handling of Women and Children Victims of Violence The implementation of Handling of Women and Children victims of violence is carried out with:

a. speed;  
b. safety and comfort;  
c. sense of empathy;  
d. non-discrimination;  
e. easy accessibility;  
f. no charge; and  
g. guaranteed confidentiality.  

#### Article 22

Forms of handling of Women and Children victims of violence as referred to in Article 21 include services:

a. complaints, consultation, and counseling;  
b. assistance;  
c. medical rehabilitation;  
d. social rehabilitation;  
e. legal; and  
f. social reintegration.

## Part Six

#### Article 23

The financing required for the implementation of Protection of Women and Children is charged to:

a. Regional Revenue and Expenditure Budget; and  
b. Other lawful and non-binding sources of funds in accordance with the provisions of applicable Laws and Regulations.

# CHAPTER III

## INTEGRATED SERVICE CENTER (PPT)

#### Article 24

(1) The provision of services to Women and Children victims of violence is carried out in an integrated manner by the PPT.

(2) The PPT as referred to in paragraph (1) is an integrated part and constitutes one unit with the PPT that provides integrated services for witnesses and/or victims of criminal acts of trafficking in persons.

(3) The provision of services to Women and Children victims of violence as referred to in paragraph (1) includes:

a. providing fast and free services to victims;  
b. providing protection and fulfillment of victims' rights to health rehabilitation, social rehabilitation, repatriation, social reintegration and legal assistance;  
c. cooperating with certain institutions in the provision of interpreters and volunteer assistants needed for victims;  
d. networking with government or private hospitals for the care and recovery of victims' health and cooperating with witness and victim protection institutions, social protection houses or trauma centers owned by the government, community or other institutions for the recovery of victims' health;  
e. providing convenience, comfort and safety for victims;  
f. maintaining the confidentiality of victims; and  
g. providing fulfillment of legal assistance for victims.

(4) Further provisions regarding the procedures and mechanisms for the implementation of PPT are regulated by Regent Regulation.

## CHAPTER IV

#### Article 25

RIGHTS OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN VICTIMS OF VIOLENCE Every Woman and Child victim of violence has the right:

a. to complaint handling;  
b. to be respected for their dignity as human beings;  
c. to receive protection from family, community, regional government and/or other parties either temporarily or based on a protection order from the court;  
d. to health and psychological recovery from the suffering experienced by the victim;  
e. to determine their own decisions;  
f. to special handling related to the confidentiality of the victim;  
g. to obtain information;  
h. to confidentiality of their identity;  
1. to social rehabilitation;  

j. to assistance by social workers and legal aid at every level of the examination process in accordance with the provisions of Laws and Regulations; and
k. to spiritual guidance services.  

#### Article 26

Women and children victims of violence in addition to having rights as referred to in Article 25, also receive special rights as follows:

a. right to survival;  
b. to grow and develop;  
c. to receive equal protection; and  
d. to participate in community life.  
